  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 936 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nBy the start of the long term we will be beginning a pattern \nchange. To our west, an upper level trough will begin its swing \nthrough the TN Valley. At the surface, an accompanying cold front \nwill be pushing east. Several days of weak WAA will be present \nalong and ahead of this system. As such, come Friday night we will\nhave an incredibly moist environment present, priming us for a \nweekend of flooding concerns.\n\nGoing into the weekend we will see dew points in the 70s and PWATs\nabove the 90th percentile per BMX sounding climatology (2\"+). This\nenvironment will be favorable for efficient rainfall production. With\npersistent forcing from Friday night through Sunday afternoon, high\nrain chances (60-80%) are in the forecast all weekend. Models\nindicate 2-4 inches of rain from Friday through Monday. Flooding\nconcerns will arise if periods of heavy rainfall cause significant\namounts of rain to fall in a short period of time. As such, WPC \nhas issued a Slight Risk (level 2/4) for excessive rainfall for \nportions of the area on Saturday into Sunday. Some uncertainty \nremains regarding the QPF totals due to the fact the front and \nparent trough will be weakening as they move through. Should the \nsystem come through weaker than currently forecast, QPF amounts \ncould trend down.\n\nThe front is currently forecast to pass during the day on Sunday, \nhowever low rain chances and cloud cover look to linger behind the \nfront through Tuesday. The lingering cloud cover paired with the slow\ninfiltration of cooler and drier air from the north will keep highs\nin the 70s and lows in the 50s for the start of the work week.",
